  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe proposed approach is to use semi-definite programming (SDP) relaxation for the non-convex optimization problem, leveraging the structure of the overlap integral and the constraints. The key insight is that the overlap integral can be expressed as a quadratic form, allowing us to formulate a convex relaxation that approximates the non-convex problem. This approach is particularly suited for problems with quadratic objectives and linear constraints. The main limitation is that the SDP relaxation may not always yield the exact global optimum, but it provides a tight upper bound.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nDefine h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1], \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1. The overlap integral C\u2085 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx can be expressed as:\n$$\nC_5 = \\max_{k} \\left( \\int h(x) dx - \\int h(x)h(x+k) dx \\right)\n$$\nThis simplifies to:\n$$\nC_5 = \\max_{k} \\left( 1 - \\int h(x)h(x+k) dx \\right)\n$$\nTo minimize C\u2085, we need to maximize the minimum autocorrelation:\n$$\n\\min_k \\int h(x)h(x+k) dx\n$$\nThe problem is convexified by replacing h(x)h(x+k) with a positive semi-definite matrix variable, enabling the use of SDP solvers.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na) Discretize h into n=100 points.\nb) Initialize h using initial_h_values if available, otherwise initialize to a uniform distribution scaled to sum=50.\nc) Formulate the SDP relaxation:\n   - Decision variables: h \u2208 \u211d^n, Y \u2208 \u211d^{n\u00d7n} (positive semi-definite)\n   - Objective: minimize C\u2085\n   - Constraints:\n     - Y = h h^T\n     - tr(Y_k) >= 1 - C\u2085 for all k\n     - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n     - sum(h) = 50\nd) Solve the SDP using CVXPY with MOSEK.\ne) Extract h from the solution and ensure feasibility.\nf) Compute the final C\u2085 using np.correlate.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nStart with n=100. Use CVXPY with MOSEK solver. Initialize Y as rank-1 matrix if possible. If the initial SDP relaxation is infeasible, try increasing n_points and transfer the parent solution.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nEstimated runtime: ~600s with CVXPY and MOSEK for n=100. Include error handling if the solver fails to converge.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nEnsure all h[i] \u2208 [0,1] and sum(h) = 50. Evaluate C\u2085 using the provided metric. Return the best h found, its C\u2085, and n_points.",
